Sogen: pseudo handles keep bit 31 clear

Sogen's pseudo handles set bit 31 (handle_value.is_pseudo), and the standard
handles are pseudo ones: the process parameters carry stdout as 0x80800008
and stdin as 0x8080000C. No handle a Windows process holds looks like that;
each is a small positive value whose low 32 bits are all that is significant.
SBCL's Windows runtime holds the standard handles in Lisp as an INDEX (a
non-negative fixnum) and passes them to C as an int (signed 32 bits), so
neither form of the value passes:

  zero-extended (Sogen as it is), at the first write to the console:
    TYPE-ERROR: The value 2155872264 is not of type (SIGNED-BYTE 32)
    when binding SB-ALIEN::VALUE
  sign-extended, before the Lisp toplevel is up:
    Internal error #116 "Object is not of type INDEX."
    ... odd fixnum: -2139095028
    internal error too early in init, can't recover

is_pseudo and is_system trade places, so a pseudo handle is a positive 32-bit
value, the same zero- or sign-extended. Nothing in the emulator reads
is_system (make_handle sets it false), and nothing names a handle bit by its
position: every pseudo handle is made by make_pseudo_handle and told by its
is_pseudo field.

diff --git a/src/windows-emulator/handles.hpp b/src/windows-emulator/handles.hpp
index 18d6092..0c4402a 100644
--- a/src/windows-emulator/handles.hpp
+++ b/src/windows-emulator/handles.hpp
@@ -55,8 +55,11 @@ namespace sogen
         uint64_t reserved : 2;
         uint64_t id : 21;
         uint64_t type : 7;
-        uint64_t is_system : 1;
+        // Bit 31 stays clear. The standard handles are pseudo ones, and a guest sees them: on Windows every
+        // handle a process holds is a small positive value whose low 32 bits are all that is significant,
+        // and a runtime may keep one in a signed 32-bit int (SBCL's does, and rejects a negative one too).
         uint64_t is_pseudo : 1;
+        uint64_t is_system : 1;
         uint64_t high_bits : 32;
     };
 
